Type of organization: Hospital and health system.
Main type of work: Hospital and clinic care across the Portland, Oregon area [83] (search: Portland).
Full or partial move: Partial (hybrid). Only the Epic disaster-recovery (backup) environment moved to Azure; the main Epic system moved to a colocation environment (its own equipment in rented data-centre space) [83] (search: colocation).
Source note: This is a 2019 vendor case study (Vendor); no more recent source was found.
Motivation (problems they were trying to solve)
Legacy Health needed its Epic electronic medical record system to be available at all times [83] (read around: Epic).
Threats included user error, equipment malfunction and the risk of a major earthquake on the Cascadia fault that runs under the Portland region [83] (search: Cascadia).
Its IS technical director said Legacy needed a completely separate disaster-recovery infrastructure outside its geographic area [83] (search: geographic).
He said that before the change, Legacy managed everything itself, down to ordering diesel fuel for the generators [83] (search: diesel).
What was done
Legacy moved the disaster-recovery environment for its Epic system to Microsoft Azure, and at the same time moved the main Epic system to a virtualized colocation environment [83] (search: colocation).
Cloud provider and services used
Microsoft Azure: Azure Virtual Machines, Azure Storage, Azure Backup and Azure SQL Database managed instances [83] (search: Azure Backup), with plans to add Azure Site Recovery [83] (search: Site Recovery).
Scale
More than 22 terabytes of data, 100 servers and several production services were moved to Azure in 11 months, a month ahead of schedule [83] (search: 11 months).
The Epic system supports 7,700 concurrent users [83] (search: 7,700).
Outcomes and results
The IS technical director said Legacy reduced the operational costs of disaster recovery by almost 65% and essentially avoided a half-million-dollar capital purchase [83] (search: 65 percent).
Before-and-after comparison
Time to add computing resources: months before, a few hours after, according to the IS technical director [83] (read around: few hours).
Disaster-recovery operating cost: almost 65% lower [83] (search: 65 percent).
Conclusion: Positive for disaster recovery (vendor-reported). Using the cloud as an off-site backup, instead of building a second data centre, gave a large reported cost reduction; the main system stayed out of the public cloud, and the figures come from Microsoft's case study.
